% What this device reads where it holds no pixel, for three pages
% cleared to three different colours. Run against a live device
% (-d <dev>) by run-device-ground-test.sh, which reads the answers of
% the whole roster and holds them against each other.
%
% This file asserts nothing. The question is whether the devices agree,
% and no single run of a single device can see that: a device asked what
% its own ground is will always say what it says. So each run reports
% its three readings and the wrapper does the comparing.
%
% erasepage clears the page to grey 1.0 (PLRM 8.2), so the colour a page
% is cleared to is not an operand of it. What that grey comes to is the
% transfer function's answer, which is how three different pages are had
% here.

/dev .privatedict /.graphicsdict get /currgstate get /device get def
/getpix { dev dup /GetPix get exec } def

% How much of the device plane one reading has to cover to be a colour.
% A device that screens what it stores holds no colour at a pixel: it
% holds a pattern over a cell that tiles the plane, and the colour is
% what a cell of it comes to. One pixel of such a device is no more its
% ground than one bit of a byte is, so the reading is taken over a whole
% cell and averaged. A device that stores the colour it is handed has a
% cell of one pixel and is read at one pixel, as it always was.
%
% The cell is asked of the device's state, which is where a screen
% installs it: what a page does to a device is kept beside the device
% rather than on it (data/device.ps).
/devst dev /.state 2 copy known { get }{ pop pop 0 dict } ifelse def
/cellw devst /.htw 2 copy known { get }{ pop pop 1 } ifelse def
/cellh devst /.hth 2 copy known { get }{ pop pop 1 } ifelse def
cellw type /integertype ne { /cellw 1 def } if
cellh type /integertype ne { /cellh 1 def } if
cellw 1 lt { /cellw 1 def } if
cellh 1 lt { /cellh 1 def } if

% Read a pixel, taking what came back rather than assuming it. How many
% components a device reports is the device's own business, and a read
% that refuses reports none; either way the stack is left at the depth
% the read was handed.
/readpix { % x y  .  [c1..cN]
    count 2 sub /rp.depth exch def
    { getpix } stopped {
        count rp.depth sub dup 0 gt { { pop } repeat }{ pop } ifelse
        [ ]
    }{
        count rp.depth sub array astore
    } ifelse
} def

% The readings are taken off the page, where there is nothing to hold a
% pixel and a mark aimed there is dropped, so what a read answers is the
% ground and nothing else. Every device answers there, whether or not it
% can report a pixel it holds.
%
% They run over one whole cell in each direction, so every place in the
% cell is read exactly once and their mean is what the cell comes to.
% Each is a whole number of pixels before the page, which is off it
% however wide the page is.
/report { % (tag) proc  .  -
    gsave settransfer erasepage grestore
    /rp.sum null def
    /rp.n 0 def
    cellw neg 1 -1 {
        /rp.x exch def
        cellh neg 1 -1 {
            rp.x exch readpix /rp.r exch def
            rp.sum null eq {
                /rp.sum rp.r def
            }{
                % a read that answers a different count from the one
                % before it is answering nothing that can be averaged
                rp.r length rp.sum length eq {
                    0 1 rp.sum length 1 sub {
                        /rp.i exch def
                        rp.sum rp.i
                            rp.sum rp.i get rp.r rp.i get add put
                    } for
                }{ /rp.sum [ ] def } ifelse
            } ifelse
            /rp.n rp.n 1 add def
        } for
    } for
    print
    rp.sum { ( ) print rp.n div cvi 20 string cvs print } forall
    (\n) print
} def

% The three pages. The first two are colours a screen's cell can carry
% exactly, and the third is not: a cell of sixteen pixels covers a grey
% to a sixteenth, so a device reading one lands a little off a device
% reading a byte, and the wrapper is left to hold each to the step its
% own channel moves in rather than to a byte's.
(GROUND full:) { } report
(GROUND high:) { pop 0.75 } report
(GROUND low:)  { pop 0.3 } report

% How many pixels one of those readings covers. It is what the reading
% can move by: a mean over a cell of n pixels moves a whole pixel of the
% cell at a time, which is the coarsest step any device here reports in.
(GROUND cell: ) print cellw cellh mul 20 string cvs print (\n) print
